{
  "behaviorChanges": [
    "`tautline merge <pr> -- <gh flags>` now applies those flags. The separator was only stripped when it came FIRST, so after an explicit PR reference it reached gh, which stops option parsing at `--` and read the flags as positionals -- merging with the default strategy instead of the requested one.",
    "`--match-head-commit` joins --repo/-R/--admin as a REFUSED passthrough flag. A caller-supplied pin replaced the commit the gate had just verified with a SHA nothing verified, overriding the race protection while recording no reason -- every other override in this verb records a decision. The verified head is now passed unconditionally.",
    "tautline test-run stops losing green runs on fast hardware. Its proof that a declared report belonged to the run compared a file mtime (kernel coarse clock) against the run's start reading (fine-grained clock), so a report written immediately after the run started could look older than the run and turn a passing suite into `predates this run` plus exit code 1. The floor now carries a tolerance for that skew, and the declared report is cleared BEFORE the command runs -- in place, as fresh-checkout mode already did -- so a command that writes no report can no longer inherit the previous run's counts. A clear that fails is its own error rather than falling through to the floor. Records already on disk are unaffected.",
    "The next-goal candidate ordering breaks ties by ascending name instead of reverse-alphabetically. Goal plans written within one timer tick share an mtime, and a fresh clone stamps every plan with one checkout time, so ties are the normal case: a repo whose plans all carry the checkout timestamp may see a different plan offered as the next goal than it did before. Nothing is written by the change in ordering."
  ],
